---
import release from "../../data/release.json";

const { latestStableVersion } = release;
const dependency = `dependencies {
    implementation("dev.nexcraft:r2d1-spring-boot-starter:${latestStableVersion}")
}`;
---

<p><strong>Available in {latestStableVersion}.</strong> The Spring Boot integration is published to Maven Central as two artifacts: the starter and its separately published auto-configuration module.</p>

<h2>Dependency</h2>
<p>Add the <code>r2d1-spring-boot-starter</code> artifact to a Spring Boot 4.0.6 application running on Java 21 or later:</p>
<pre><code class="language-kotlin">{dependency}</code></pre>

<p>The starter depends on the separately published <code>r2d1-spring-boot-autoconfigure</code> module. Use the starter for normal applications; use the auto-configuration module directly when composing a custom starter.</p>

<h2>What it configures</h2>
<p>Set <code>r2d1.enabled=true</code> to activate the integration. The configuration supports R2 or filesystem document storage and D1 or JDBC indexing, while preserving the framework-neutral R2D1 collection API.</p>
<p>JDBC uses platform-thread execution by default. Select virtual-thread execution explicitly only on a Java 25 runtime; the integration does not provide a fallback for older runtimes.</p>

<h2>Resource ownership</h2>
<p>Existing application <code>DataSource</code>, <code>Executor</code>, <code>S3AsyncClient</code>, and <code>HttpClient</code> beans are reused when selected by name or as the unique or <code>@Primary</code> bean. Application-owned resources are not closed by the integration. Resources created by the integration are closed with its managed beans.</p>

<h2>Scope</h2>
<p>Spring Data repositories, transactions, AOP, controllers, retry and compensation policies, R2DBC, server lifecycle, and connection-pool management are outside these modules. Native-image hints are not included until a concrete Spring AOT requirement is verified.</p>
